\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{One law for every block level}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{There is a unique probability measure on
#{\prod_{B\ge 1}\mathbb R^B} whose finite-dimensional
distributions are the centered Gaussian laws with the
counting covariance operator of \ref{fdip-00BO}. Whenever #{r} divides #{B},
the mean of #{r} consecutive coordinates at level #{B}
equals the coordinate at level #{B/r}, almost surely. These
countably many identities hold on one common set of full measure.}

\proof{
\p{Each block-mean law at finite #{M} is centered Gaussian, as
a linear image of the mesh field. For every
#{\xi\in\mathbb R^B}, let #{h} be the counting inner product
on #{\mathbb R^B}. The characteristic function of the
block-mean law is
#{\ue^{-\frac{1}{2}h(\xi,C_M\xi)}}, through the counting
covariance pairing, at an admissible mesh size #{n=BM\ge 3}.
Pointwise convergence
of these functions to a continuous limit determines the
limiting law, and that law is the centered Gaussian with the
covariance of \ref{fdip-00BO}. Index the
coordinates by pairs
#{(B,b)} with #{B\ge 1} and #{b=0,\ldots,B-1}. For a finite
set of these coordinates, let #{B_*} be a common multiple of
the levels that appear. The joint law is the image of the
level-#{B_*} Gaussian under the averaging maps, not merely the
separate marginal law at each level. At every
finite #{M}, \ref{fdip-00BN} identifies a coarse block mean
with the average of the fine ones. Both sides converge in
distribution, so the identity holds for the limiting
Gaussians, and enlarging
the finite set does not change the marginal. Each factor is
the Borel line #{\mathbb R}, second-countable and completely
pseudo-metrizable, and each finite-dimensional law is a
probability measure.}
\p{Mathlib revision \code{727d8bbe6044} supplies the extension
for that class of spaces.
\leanref{MeasureTheory.isProjectiveLimit_projectiveLimit}
constructs a projective limit,
\leanref{MeasureTheory.isProbabilityMeasure_projectiveLimit}
makes it a probability measure, and
\leanref{MeasureTheory.IsProjectiveLimit.unique} gives
uniqueness among finite measures with those projections.
These three projective-limit references remain partial machinery.
They are not a Gaussian or Green-kernel formalization.
The declarations take the finite-dimensional laws as data.
The covariances are the content of \ref{fdip-00BO}, and the
identification with averaged integrals of #{G} is the content
of \ref{fdip-00BP}. Every finite collection
of levels satisfies the averaging identities, so the
identities hold for the projective limit.}
}
